<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Make Connections - Eigen Review</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Two convenient Sage tools.
            If <c>A</c> is a square matrix, then
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <c>A.eigenvalues()</c> gives a list of the eigenvalues,
                        and
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <c>(A - lambda*identity_matrix(n)).right_kernel()</c>
                        gives a basis of the null space <m>\nul(A-\lambda I)</m>
                        if <m>n</m> is the number of rows and columns of
                        <m>A</m> and <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue.
                        How does this help you find eigenvectors?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the matrix
                <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{rr}
    -2 \amp 8 \\
    2 \amp -2 \\
  \end{array}\right]</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and a basis for each of the
                    eigenspaces.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is <m>A</m> diagonalizable?
                    If so, find <m>P</m> and <m>D</m> and verify that
                    <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a diagonalization of <m>A^3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a diagonalization of <m>A^{-1}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find <m>\det(A)</m> and explain why <m>\det(A)</m> equals
                    the product of the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                We know that the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> are the roots of the
                characteristic equation <m>{\det(A-\lambda I) = 0}</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>\lambda = 0</m> is an eigenvalue, what does this say
                    about <m>\det(A)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What does this say about the invertibility of <m>A</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How do the eigenvalues of a matrix tell us about its
                    invertibility?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Last class we looked at the transition matrix
                <m>A=\left[\begin{array}{cc}0.8\amp0.6\\0.2\amp0.4\end{array}\right]</m>
                applied to trucks at two locations.
                We wanted to know the long-term behavior of an initial state
                <m>\xvec_0</m> that evolves as <m>\xvec_{k+1}=A\xvec_k</m>.
                In other words, what happened to <m>A^k\xvec_0</m> as <m>k</m>
                grows large.
                We found that <m>A</m> had eigenvalues <m>1</m> and <m>0.2</m>,
                and the
                <m>\lim_\limits{k\rightarrow\infty} A^k = \left[\begin{array}{cc}0.75\amp0.75\\0.25\amp0.25\end{array}\right]</m>.
                So if we start with 1000 trucks total so that <m>P+Q=1000</m>,
                then in the long-term we have
                <md>
                    <mrow>
                    \lim_{k\rightarrow\infty} A^k = \amp \left[\begin{array}{cc}0.75\amp0.75\\0.25\amp0.25\end{array}\right]\twovec{P}{Q} 
                 </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                    = \amp P\twovec{0.75}{0.25}+Q\twovec{0.75}{0.25} 
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                    = \amp 1000\twovec{0.75}{0.25} 
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                    = \amp \twovec{750}{250},
                    </mrow>
                </md>
                so there will be 750 trucks at location <m>P</m> and 250 at
                location <m>Q</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's look at a different transition matrix:
                    <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}
    0 \amp 1 \\
    1 \amp 0 \\
  \end{bmatrix}</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Find the eigenvalues of <m>B</m> and bases for its
                    eigenspaces.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a diagonalization of <m>B</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens when you look at <m>B^k</m> as <m>k</m> grows?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is different about the eigenvalues of <m>A</m> and
                    <m>B</m> that distinguishes the long-term behavior?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <outcomes>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Make sure you can answer the following questions:
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ul>
            <li>How do eigenvalues relate to the invertibility of a matrix?</li>
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>